A||N||53||bHooper||are there any Animal Doctors around?||||5||
Z||000000||bHooper||05-30-2001||08:29 PM||||i have a problem with my Pooch.  she's a 3 year old Border Collie.  several weeks ago, i took her into the vet to get her shots and have her checked out, cause it seemed as if her eating habits had changed (back to picking) and she had breathing difficulties (we also thought she was constipated, but as soon as the vet got her she went, so that wasnt a problem)<P>anyway, so the vet tells me she's 5-10 lbs over weight and lets her go.  bring her home and everything "seems" ok, until last week when Melinda and i decide to take the dogs for a short walk around the neighborhood (about 1 mile).  well, fat Byng all but is about to die when we get in... panting, panting, panting... for 1 hour!   her tongue got very red, i attributed this to the weight condition (the panting that is).<P>so here we are last night, and she wakes me up with her breathing (always has snored), a very labored breathing... akin to 90 yo ex smoker on the o2 at the hospital.  quick, wheezy, labored breathing!  this AM, when i got up she appeared to be fine again, so i went off to do what i had to do.  <P>when i get home, and play with her some, i notice that she has this labored breathing thing going again.<P>any thoughts? <P>thanks!<BR>hoop<P>------------------<BR>hoop<BR>Red '86 GT 5 speed<BR>*Black '86 SE Automatic||||reg||5
Z||000001||isthiswhereiputausername?||05-30-2001||09:07 PM||||Hello...I'm more of a cats person, but I have the same problem with one of my cats.  He's 19lbs and has labored breathing. He doesn't do it all the time, but when he does, it worries me.  My mother had a cat that did the same thing and she thought it was asthma (but she found out that it wasn't...it was just something harmless that the cat did).<P>I would check with the vet again as soon as possible and see about getting your dog on a weight reducing diet (maybe Hill's Science Diet Prescription for Weight Reducing) and also ask the vet if your dog could have asthma or allergies that could be causing respiratory problems.<P>One thing about vets, they are not always right and can be like human doctors, in a hurry to rush the patient out the door, so be persistent and ask lots of questions...get another opinion from another vet if you have to.  Also, research it on the Internet.  Maybe try going to <A HREF="http://www.mamma.com" TARGET=_blank>www.mamma.com</A>  (search engine) and do a search for breathing problems with dogs.  There are a lot of pet websites out there that have message boards and a lot of pet lovers that would love to help answer your questions...there may be some out there that are experiencing the same problem and have some good advice for you!<P>Take Care of yourself and Byng!<P>Rachel||||reg||1
Z||000002||LZeitgeist||05-30-2001||09:09 PM||||Hoop - <P>Well, in order to run, a dog needs three things.. fuel, air and spark. So, if you've checked her spark plugs and they're firing...<P>Oh, waitaminute... sorry 'bout that..  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P><BR>I don't have any medical advice to give you, but take notes of when, how often and under what conditions she's having trouble, and call the vet to let them know what trouble she's having, and ask if they can suggest a course of action... <P>I'll be keeping my fingers crossed for all of you... <BR>||||reg||1
Z||000003||JSocha||05-30-2001||09:34 PM||||I'm not a vet, but I think 7 dogs, 7 cats and soon to be 8 horses (current status) gives me some background in the pet situation.  Family history in pets here to.<P>Being you are from NC, don't know what the mosquitoes are like down there, but my uncle had a dog that was only two when she passed away.  She started having difficulties breathing, dog was being treated for asthma.  Dog acted like it would suddenly have asthma attacks.  Finally, she passed away cause she couldn't breath.<P>They did an autopsy on my uncles dog, and the heart was just packed full of HEART WORMS.  She couldn't get enough oxygen.<P>My recommendation, take your collie to another vet (may have to get copies of your dogs records before you go).  Ask other people how they feel not only about your vet but the other one you may take your dog to.<P>Our vet isn't worth sh*t here in town and a year ago I lost my dog of nine years because he wouldn't examine her like I asked him to when she started to collapse.  She fell head first off the arm rest of our couch and I believe she did something to her neck.  Because she would only collapse when she tried to move it and she was stiff after her fall.  I couldn't get him to take an xray of her even after I explained this started happening after that incident and all of a sudden.<P>------------------<BR>     <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/jsocha_2.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000004||bHooper||05-30-2001||10:43 PM||||nope, checked the doctors report... negatory on the annual heartworm test.  he did recomend heartworm refill (gotta plug the sponsors!)  <P>he did comment that she had "mild" ulcers in the mouth... what should i look for there?<P><P>------------------<BR>hoop<BR>Red '86 GT 5 speed<BR>*Black '86 SE Automatic||||reg||1
Z||000005||Formula88||05-31-2001||09:50 AM||||Hoop, Oberlin Road Animal Hospital in Raleigh.  I'd take the Pope's dogs there.  He's a dog lover himself, and has some some incredible surgery on a friend of mine's show Irish Setter (alas, she didn't survive, but that was because the damage was too sever - from another incompetent vet who left a needle inside her after surgery  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/mad.gif"> )  I don't remember his name, but the Oberlin Road Animal Hospital is his practice.  I believe he's the only vet there.  If I ever have another dog (when I get a yard for one to play in) he's the only vet I'll be going to.<P>Good luck.||||reg||6
Z||000006||JSocha||05-31-2001||10:48 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Formula88:<BR><B>...and has some some incredible surgery on a friend of mine's show Irish Setter (alas, she didn't survive, but that was because the damage was too sever - from another incompetent vet who left a needle inside her after surgery   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/mad.gif"> )....</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>In other words, "We are happy to say the operation we performed was a complete success, however the patient died."<P>------------------<BR>     <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/jsocha_2.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000007||bHooper||05-31-2001||05:43 PM||||thats too funny!  almost as funny as me driving a hour and a half to take the dog to the damn vet!<P><P>------------------<BR>hoop<BR>Red '86 GT 5 speed<BR>*Black '86 SE Automatic||||reg||1
Z||000008||bHooper||06-01-2001||07:22 PM||||so, here's the update... the dog has fluid in her chest, and it is "compressing her lungs"... dont know where it came from or what's causing it, as all test are negative.  they gave her a shot that causes her to go to the bathroom a lot, and me a bottle of pills (for the dog) to giver her for the same thing, as well as an antibiotic to "cure" the problem.<P>the first xray showed a shadow of a lung, behind all of the fluid.  the second one (after the shot) showed much more of the lung.  <P>anyway, we left (with our bottles of pills), stopped and got a chickens foot and a glocve of garlic for her to wear on her collar.  <P>we are supposed to go back to get another xray in 2 weeks to see how the pills have done!<P>hoop||||reg||1
Z||000009||Raydar||06-01-2001||07:59 PM||||Best of luck, Hoop!||||reg||1
Z||000010||bHooper||06-21-2001||08:56 AM||||Well, we had the 2nd xray last week, and it did show some improvement in the chest cavity... but not enough.  <P>The options given at that time were:<BR>1) stronger antibiotic<BR>2) take a fluid sample<BR>or 3) do an ultra sound on the heart.<P>I chose one, as there was some improvement and I felt we were headed on the right track.  However my wife (fiance' at the time) and her mother decided to take Byng back to the vet to have the fluid sample taken, and sent off for testing.<P>On Monday, we got the results... nothing had grown in the culture.  You don't know how upsetting that is to hear!<P>This morning, Melinda dropped the dog off for the ultra sound test.  I am very confident that my 3.5 yo dog has a heart problem.  I found some info on the net that seems to back up a lot of the symptoms. <A HREF="http://www.geocities.com/Heartland/Plains/8870/heart.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.geocities.com/Heartland/Plains/8870/heart.html</A> <P>you don't know how depressing it is to watch your dog have problems (without pain).  The thought of loosing my puppy now lingers in my mind.<P> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif"><P><P>------------------<BR>hoop<BR>Red '86 GT 5 speed<BR>*Black '86 SE Automatic||||reg||1
Z||000011||87ECO5||06-21-2001||12:21 PM||||Hoop - our thoughts and prayers are with you, Melinda and Byng!<P>I understand exactly what you're going through, as our 8-year-old golden retriever is being killed slowly by the severe seizures she continues to have - despite the abundance of medication we're giving her!    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif"><P>Let us know if there's anything we can do ...||||reg||9
Z||000012||bHooper||06-21-2001||12:39 PM||||thanks Scott!  Melinda and I look forward to meeting you and the Mrs., next month.<P>I am still waiting to hear from the vet... it has been a long 2 hours since I last talked with them.<P>------------------<BR>hoop<BR>Red '86 GT 5 speed<BR>*Black '86 SE Automatic||||reg||1
Z||000013||JSocha||06-21-2001||12:39 PM||||Hoop, hope all is going well and the ultra sound gives you more of an idea what is going on so it can hopefully be corrected.<P>I know what its like to love a dog (we have 7, lost mine of 9 years last May and saved another from being put down just a couple days later), care for em to no ends.<P>Keep us posted as to the results of the ultrasound.<P>Hopefully Byng is not suffering from this.<P>------------------<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/jsocha_2.gif">  <p>[This message has been edited by JSocha (edited 06-21-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000014||87ECO5||06-21-2001||03:32 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by bHooper:<BR><B>thanks Scott!  Melinda and I look forward to meeting you and the Mrs., next month.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>You are very welcome and we are looking forward to meeting you two as well.<P>We'll be thinking about you guys ... [Scott giving Brian a virtual pat on the back] ...||||reg||1
Z||000015||bHooper||06-21-2001||06:46 PM||||... well we now know wahts wrong with Byng.  it's called  cirrhosis affusion.  basically it is a rupture in one of the tubes or ducts that transport fat throughout the body.  they do not know where it is, or how to cure it.  exploratory surgury is an option, but an expensive one, as they need to look for the rupture.  <P>a change in diet, combined with weekly or daily fluid draining is the other option (at the moment, the doc is still investigating this).<P>basically my dog is not going to be around for ever, as i don't have the $ to spend over $1000 more on the dog.  at least i feel better knowing what it is, and knowing that it's not the heart!<P><P>------------------<BR>hoop<BR>Red '86 GT 5 speed<BR>*Black '86 SE Automatic||||reg||1
Z||000016||87ECO5||06-22-2001||10:21 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by bHooper:<BR><B>a change in diet, combined with weekly or daily fluid draining is the other option (at the moment, the doc is still investigating this).</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>That doesn't sound very pleasant (for you two <I>OR</I> Byng)!  Keep us informed ...||||reg||1
Z||000017||bHooper||06-26-2001||06:20 PM||||we took Byng in to get tapped, today.  they drained 2 liters of fluid out of her, and exrayed and looked at her with ultrasound.  it appears that she may have a hearniated diaphram, which caused the liver to shift and one of the ducts to tear.<P>i know that sounds bad, but actually it's good... it may be fixable, albeit $2000 worth of fixable!<P>stay tuned, there may be a fiero for sale soon!<P>either way, i am feeling much better!<P>hoop||||reg||1
Z||000018||frontal lobe||06-26-2001||11:30 PM||||I had a human who I had to tap weekly for about 1 1/2 years.  Took 6-8 liters off his chest once a week!  Put an iv catheter in his chest, hooked it up to iv tubing, stuck the open end of the iv tubing in a big basin, got him comfortable on a bed with something to read, and let it drain until it was dry!  True story. ||||reg||1
Z||000019||Formula88||06-27-2001||09:57 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by JSocha:<BR><B> In other words, "We are happy to say the operation we performed was a complete success, however the patient died."<P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Yeah, well, what happened was her Irish Setter *****  bloated (common problem in Irish Setters) and required emergency surgery.  Well, the bonehead surgeon left a sucher needle inside her and it pierced her liver, and a few other things.  It almost came out through her torso, by the time we got her to Oberlin Road Animal Hospital.  The doc there was able to fix the damage, but she had to be under for so long, and was so weakened by the damage, that she died in recovery.  And this was a 2 year old show dog with BIG potential.  She was sired by one of the nations top dogs. (National show winner - etc.).  You can bet there was a huge lawsuit.  Not only the loss of Megan, but the loss of her pups (conservatively valued at $5000 each, figure the average litter and number of litters over her lifetime)  That's big money, folks.    I broke up with the owner (we were quasi-dating at the time) before she got the settlement, but her plan was to erect a monument to Megan at the NC Vet School as a warning to other vets - so hopefully this can be avoided in the future.||||reg||1
Z||000020||87ECO5||06-27-2001||10:00 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by bHooper:<BR><B>i know that sounds bad, but actually it's good... it may be fixable, albeit $2000 worth of fixable!</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Not many people would spend that kind of money on their dog.  I'm glad to hear you're considering it.  I know I would have spent it on Maxi had I known it would have helped her.<P>Still on for 7/9?||||reg||14
Z||000021||JSocha||06-27-2001||03:20 PM||||Formula88, didn't mean my post to sound the way it did.  I just feel that sometimes the vets need to get their @rse holes kicked.<P>Parents had one of their dogs that they took in for emergency surgery, nothing life threatening.  Show German Shepard, Shintzenhound trained in Germany and sired by Ubaron, a prize show dog.  She died a week after the operation.<P>The vet came out saying that the surgery was successful.  Parents were elated and eventually took the dog home.  <P>However, our vet left something inside, she became infected throughout her blood stream and died.  So they took her to another vet to find out why, and found out they had left guaze inside her after the surgery and something else that cut her internally.<P>Off the animals, my mom went in for surgery for her teeth/jaw.  The Dr. left a needle in her cheek from the anastsia or during the procedure.  She came home and her cheek became infected a few days later.<P>She went to the emergency room, where they took an xray and here was a needle.  She was in the ICU for a few days thereafter because of the infection.<P>The surgeon after her operation apparently knew that an item was unaccounted for, but they didn't check into it any further.<P>Another day, and my mom could have died.<P>IE, my comment.  Sorry if it offended you as I know where they/you were coming from.  I wasn't trying to be humourse as I have 7 dogs myself, one which I lost last year.  And I think if I would have made the vet do the x-ray like I wanted him to do, she more then likely would either be here still or it would put some closure to my belief of why she died.<P>------------------<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/jsocha_2.gif"> ||||reg||9
Z||000022||bHooper||06-27-2001||03:48 PM||||as for the dog, we still haven't made up our mind yet.  $2000 is a lot of money, especially when i was downsized back in april.  according to our vet, there are two people that he would want to perform the operation.  the downside to this fella, is that he is on vacation and won't be back until next week sometime (while Melinda and I plan to go out of town for 10 days tomorrow!).<P>the first, is on vacation.  but he used to work at the after hours clinic, and is well experienced with delicate surgury.  he will also use all of the info that our vet has collected (the x-ray's and ultra sound scans).  the upside with this crowd is that they could probably do it on friday (albeit at a greater expense (probably $1000 more, total, than the other people!).<P>their is a 3rd option, leave the dog with our friends and wait until we get back to talk with the first doctor.  <P>as for the 9th, i don't see a problem, unless we have to go to the vet for something.  you have my phone numbers, right?  if so call me sunday to let me know when you will be coming through.<P><BR>the second surgeon, is from the new "specialty clinic" in town.  you can only go there by referal.  while i don't doubt that they are fully capable of performing the operation, they refuse to use anyone else's referance material.  meaning, that they will want to do their own x-rays and ultra sound.  that would be an additional $400 charge, basically to tell us if they can or can't do the surgury!<P>------------------<BR>hoop<BR>Red '86 GT 5 speed<BR>*Black '86 SE Automatic||||reg||1
Z||000023||JSocha||06-27-2001||04:07 PM||||Don't know what the vet schools are like down or around there, however, have you talked to your vet about having your beloved pooch operated on for the necessary ailment by college/university people.<P>More experience, shadowed by knowledgeable professors and less costly.<P>My dads poodle Boo-Boo while playing with the other dogs had a tendon in his neck snap to which he was then paralyzed.  Knowing the risks involved both by a local vet and the university regarding an operation, my dad finally opted to have it done at the veterinarian school in Minnisota versus by any vet.  They stated it clearly that no dog had either survived this type of operation or lived long afterwards and typically was paralyzed to the point they couldn't eat.  Boo-Boo lived three more years, actually walked again but not like he used to, but the wires used eventualy cut through the vertebrae where he ended up having a second operation.  Thereafter, he finally was put to sleep after we begged my father to do so to end the suffering he was now enduring as he was gaing weight and it was a struggle.<P>------------------<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/jsocha_2.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000024||bHooper||06-27-2001||04:22 PM||||well, we just talked with the more expensive option.  they weren't terrribly interested in looking at our vets info.  they said that their diagnostic would run to the tune of $4-600, plus $1500-2000 for the surgury itself.  as for survivability, the odds are at %50.<P>those are low odds, and high dollars.  we are going ot tell our vet to talk with option one.<P>however, it is looking like we may extend our dogs life just long enough to say goodbye (as long as it doesn't suffer!)<P><P>------------------<BR>hoop<BR>Red '86 GT 5 speed<BR>*Black '86 SE Automatic||||reg||1
Z||000025||Formula88||06-28-2001||09:57 AM||||Sounds like that specialty clinic needs to get over themselves.  Wanting to do your own x-rays etc. is fine, but to not even be interested in looking at what's already there?  Sounds stoopid to me. <P>Good luck, Hoop.||||reg||1
Z||000026||fierobaby||06-28-2001||11:06 AM||||well I hope your dog will be ok I wounder if this is a common thing in Border Collies mine had the same problem. He was fine lived a ful 7 more years after his problem he was part malamute though so he was a huge dog 130LBS big big doggy <P>------------------<BR>1984 Sweet Candy Apple red Fiero sport coupe :-D<BR>    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/cfiero.JPG"> ||||reg||1
Z||000027||bHooper||06-30-2001||09:54 AM||||so, did your dog have a herniated diagphram?!  and it lived for 7 years, after diagnosis?!   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>man, you don't know how good that would make me feel if she could live a healthy life, if surgury wasn't an option!<P>let me know.<P>thanks!<BR>hoop||||reg||1
Z||000028||frontal lobe||07-04-2001||01:32 AM||||I just sent you an e-mail.  Hope you get it soon.<P>There are different degrees of diaphragmatic herniation, and so I wouldn't see your dog living 7 happy years like the other fellow's dog based on what you describe of how she is doing and how much fluid had to be removed.  Sorry, but just want you to have realistic expectations.<P>If they only have to fix the diaphragm, the success rate can be pretty high.  If they had to try to repair the liver or any duct work, that is a lot tougher.<P>Good luck to you and your dog.||||reg||1
Z||000029||bHooper||07-21-2001||09:10 AM||||on Friday, we dropped Byng off for surgury.  we were under the impression that she had a herniated diaphragm and a kaylus affusion (the leaking duct).<P>under the old saying of "you never know what you got until you open it up"... the doctor found an intact diaphragm... and what appears to be Lymphoma Sarcoma!<P>my 3.5 yo dog is apparently terminal!  the doc sent a sample off for testing... however, if it comes back positive, there's not much that can be done.  meds can only give her a year, at most, and i'm not sure that can do that.<P>anyway, there's not much of a better way to say "have a shitty weekend" than to be told your dog is terminal!<P>ugh!<P>------------------<BR>hoop<BR><FONT SIZE="3"><FONT COLOR="red"><B>Red</B></FONT></FONT> 86 GT 5 speed<BR><FONT SIZE="3"><B>Black</B></FONT> <u>86 SE Automatic</u>||||reg||1
Z||000030||mrfiero||07-21-2001||01:19 PM||||That's a shame......nothing worse than finding out your beloved pet is not going to be around much longer.<P>Several years ago my mothers cat was acting strange (getting bloated, etc.) she took her to the vet and found out it was FIP (feline infectious perontinitis).  The vet then asked my mother when she wanted to schedule the euthanasia.  My mom was floored she said no way and took her cat home.  The next day, however, it became obvious that she had to be put to sleep.....her body was squeezing her insides out (I'll leave it at that.....not a pleasant sight).<P>We also had a Malamute that was happy and healthy for the 11 years we had it.  Then one day he just didn't move......never got up from his bed area.  It was a Friday and he deteriorated badly over the weekend.  Brought him to the vet on Monday to run some tests and whatnot.  Come to find find out his kidneys had failed and there was nothing we could do for him!  That sucked......he was healthy one day and near death/dead the next.     <P>Good luck and at least you can say you did everything you could to save your dog.  I know I couldn't live with myself if I didn't at least attempt to help one of my pets when they were sick.....we spent close to $1000 on the Malamute (tests, etc.) trying to prolong it's life.  Even though they failed we felt good knowing we did all we could do.<P>Take care and give Byng LOTS of attention/loving while you still have her. <P>||||reg||9
Z||000031||Lady Pegasus||07-21-2001||01:59 PM||||I'm very sorry about your dog.  It's hard when you don't know what to do for your pet.  All I can offer is this...make her as comfortable as possible for the time that you have her.  Don't let her life drag on when it should end.  You don't want the last thoughts of your pet to be of her deteriorated condition.  And understand this, as well...vets try VERY hard to understand their patients.  I think being a veterinarian is one of the most difficult medical professions there is.  Your patients can't tell you what is wrong with them, unlike we humans can.  Just enjoy your pet while you still have her, and I pray things will be alright for you.  Just remember the good, not the bad.<P>------------------<BR>Hey pretty....don't you wanna take a ride with me...through my world?||||reg||1
Z||000032||Mach10||07-21-2001||05:39 PM||||**EDIT****<BR>ACK! Wrong post-smilie. That should NOT have been a happy-face. Sorry.<BR>******<P>My Girlfriend's dog George, a yappy Maltese cross was fine, except for chronic sinus trouble (Wouldn't stop snorting. Gross   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/tongue.gif">), and then got sick. And sicker. And sicker. They took him in, and he turned out to have Diabetes. So they started on Insulin injections and a modified diet. He was doing well, up till the relatives came to visit. He had a habit of biting people he didn't like (i.e everyone ouside of the live-in residents), so they shut him up in the master bedroom. After a couple of hours, he found a way into the closet, or more specifically the junkfood therein. After chowing down on most of a 1kg slab of brownie, (this is a 10lb dog) he went to sleep. 4 hours later, after vomiting profusely, started convulsing. At the animal hospital, he went into a coma. The doctors managed to stabilize him, but he had almost no renal function left, and was almost certainly blind. They made the decision to end it there. <BR>Pets rule until you lose them   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif"><p>[This message has been edited by Mach10 (edited 07-21-2001).]||||reg||7
Z||000033||Cliff Pennock||07-21-2001||08:50 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>meds can only give her a year, at most, and i'm not sure that can do that.[/B]<HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>That's very sad to hear. Not to get your hopes up, but I used to have a dog that was diagnosed with a terminal desease as well when he was only 1 and a half years old and they gave him 3 months at most. He lived for another 5 years.||||reg||1
Z||000034||bHooper||07-21-2001||08:55 PM||||oh man, that's rough!  my dog has had it easy... that's the tough part.  i have been so good to this dog.  the only human food that she has ever gotten, is what falls on the floor.  she gets fed a good brand of dog food, never been abused... always treated 1st class.<P>this is the first dog that i ahd ever chosen, and raised on my on.  Byng is well behaved (leaves the room when you put food on a plate), taken cared of and loved.  in return she is a very loving and fun dog.<P> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Mvc-004s_9.jpg"> <BR>and<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Mvc-007s_2.jpg"> <P>------------------<BR>hoop<BR><FONT SIZE="3"><FONT COLOR="red"><B>Red</B></FONT></FONT> 86 GT 5 speed<BR><FONT SIZE="3"><B>Black</B></FONT> <u>86 SE Automatic</u>||||reg||9
